{
  "case_id": "2010-09-1205",
  "document_hash": "13fa1aa1581158f10c6da4a73e741103b93e974e09312a7c6b9c36f2d5f30d10",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners",
      "case_id": "2010-09-1205",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "13fa1aa1581158f10c6da4a73e741103b93e974e09312a7c6b9c36f2d5f30d10",
      "evidence_id": "ev-13fa1aa15811-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Petitioners Keith Smith and Shirley Sperlazza were not named plaintiffs in the prior Federal proceeding litigated by George McCollins. They never received notice of that prior proceeding; they never received an opportunity to appear and be heard; they never received an opportunity to opt out; and they never received an opportunity to appeal the decision denying class certification."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2010-09-1205",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "13fa1aa1581158f10c6da4a73e741103b93e974e09312a7c6b9c36f2d5f30d10",
      "evidence_id": "ev-13fa1aa15811-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: Well, suppose a class action based on diversity is filed in one of the Federal districts in West Virginia, and the district court denies class certification. The same plaintiff, the same plaintiff's attorney, takes the old complaint, writes in the name of the new named party, files exactly the same complaint in another Federal -- in the other Federal district in West Virginia. Would your argument be the same? That can go forward, get another shot at class certification?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2010-09-1205",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "13fa1aa1581158f10c6da4a73e741103b93e974e09312a7c6b9c36f2d5f30d10",
      "evidence_id": "ev-13fa1aa15811-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: All you have to do is get a new named plaintiff?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners",
      "case_id": "2010-09-1205",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "13fa1aa1581158f10c6da4a73e741103b93e974e09312a7c6b9c36f2d5f30d10",
      "evidence_id": "ev-13fa1aa15811-0000-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 7,
        "start": 7
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. MONAHAN: That's -- that's true, Your Honor. We submit, however, that any procedural rights which have been recognized and adopted -- those procedural rights, just as the substantive claim itself, have to be adjudicated consistent with due process. And West Virginia itself has adopted Rule 23 of the West Virginia Rules of Civil Procedure, and that State has the right to apply and interpret that rule as it sees fit to manage its own docket and administrate its own docket as it sees fit."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2010-09-1205",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "13fa1aa1581158f10c6da4a73e741103b93e974e09312a7c6b9c36f2d5f30d10",
      "evidence_id": "ev-13fa1aa15811-0000-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 8,
        "start": 8
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAGAN: Well, Mr. Monahan, do you mean by that that you would have a blanket rule that a decision on Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 23 can never be preclusive as to a State Rule of Civil Procedure 23?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2010-09-1205",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "13fa1aa1581158f10c6da4a73e741103b93e974e09312a7c6b9c36f2d5f30d10",
      "evidence_id": "ev-13fa1aa15811-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAGAN: When -- when Congress\nenacted CAFA, did Congress think about this precise\nissue, the issue that Justice Ginsburg is raising about\na lawyer going from State to State with a different\nnamed plaintiff? Was that -- was that part of what\nCongress was reacting to?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MR. MONAHAN",
      "case_id": "2010-09-1205",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "13fa1aa1581158f10c6da4a73e741103b93e974e09312a7c6b9c36f2d5f30d10",
      "evidence_id": "ev-13fa1aa15811-0001-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. MONAHAN: Your Honor, this Court has\nnoted a procedural right to seek class certification.\nThere is no right to have one."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2010-09-1205",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "13fa1aa1581158f10c6da4a73e741103b93e974e09312a7c6b9c36f2d5f30d10",
      "evidence_id": "ev-13fa1aa15811-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: Did it ever happen -- did\nyou ever come across an instance before where in a\nFederal court a judge in the district court says, no,\nyou can't have a certification, no; and then a different\nplaintiff went to a different Federal court in a\ndifferent part of the country and asked for a similar\ncertification? Have you ever found anything like that\nin precedent, that it's in two Federal courts rather\nthan the State/Federal?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MR. MONAHAN",
      "case_id": "2010-09-1205",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "13fa1aa1581158f10c6da4a73e741103b93e974e09312a7c6b9c36f2d5f30d10",
      "evidence_id": "ev-13fa1aa15811-0001-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. MONAHAN: Yes, Your Honor. I believe\nthat maybe Thorogood, the recent Thorogood case out of\nthe Seventh Circuit, might involve something similar to\nthat."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2010-09-1205",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "13fa1aa1581158f10c6da4a73e741103b93e974e09312a7c6b9c36f2d5f30d10",
      "evidence_id": "ev-13fa1aa15811-0001-0006",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 25,
        "start": 25
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: Of course, you -- you would\nsay that the subsequent plaintiff is not bound if he was\nnot given notice and an opportunity to opt out, even if\nhe came back to the same court, right?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MR. BECK",
      "case_id": "2010-09-1205",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "13fa1aa1581158f10c6da4a73e741103b93e974e09312a7c6b9c36f2d5f30d10",
      "evidence_id": "ev-13fa1aa15811-0001-0007",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 25,
        "start": 25
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. BECK: Mr. Chief Justice, and may it\nplease the Court:\nThe core issue here is whether absent class\nmembers can be bound by a denial of class certification\nwhere there was adequate representation on that issue\nbut not notice and opportunity to be heard."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2010-09-1205",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "13fa1aa1581158f10c6da4a73e741103b93e974e09312a7c6b9c36f2d5f30d10",
      "evidence_id": "ev-13fa1aa15811-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 37,
        "start": 37
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Oh, but why isn't it really, because here what happened was that there was a denial of class certification and there was a granting of a summary judgment motion at one and the same time? But the thing that was responsible for getting the case out of court was the granting of the summary judgment motion, not the denial of class certification. That was extraneous to the judgment that the case was dismissed."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2010-09-1205",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "13fa1aa1581158f10c6da4a73e741103b93e974e09312a7c6b9c36f2d5f30d10",
      "evidence_id": "ev-13fa1aa15811-0002-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 43,
        "start": 43
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I'm -- I'm the -- I'm the party trying to bring the later class action, and you tell me I can't do it because somebody else sought a class action, and -- and it -- and it was denied."
    },
